====Usage notes====
* Some dictionaries and many older grammars put ''several'' into the word class 'pronoun' in many of its uses.
* To some speakers, ''several'' means "more than one<!--ref: M-W--> but not very many"; to some others, it means "more than two but not very many." The sole difference is whether two things count as ''several'', when the counting is not precise (especially in situations where it may change at any instant, such as ''several birds in the yard''). A similar variability likewise applies to ''[[a few#Determiner|a few]]'', as well as ''a [[couple#English:_two like items, considered together|couple]] of'' versus ''a [[couple#English:_small number|couple]] of''.

